00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.940
to get you started and also
how you can use it to change.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:00.180
So like a very
quick introduction,

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:04.890
the crash course to css. So what
is? css is basically

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:09.389
how the internet
describes the way

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:12.120
that elements are
displayed on a page.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.600
Basically, it lets you
control multiple elements

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.140
by the usage of
classes and selectors.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:22.620
And basically, it's made
of very small instructions,

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:27.040
which are called statements.
Statements simply do two things.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:31.050
So they identify an
object on the page

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.350
and they declare a value
for a specific property

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:35.400
of an element.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.750
So this is a very
simple example of what

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:39.940
this css statement would look like.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:41.790
So you can see that
we're styling something

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:44.340
that has a class of
search box and we're

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.590
styling the border
into the background

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.237
of that specific element.

00:02:48.237 --> 00:02:50.070
The cool thing, with
css is that if you

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:52.740
have two elements, certain
elements, or 50 elements

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:54.870
with this specific
class, they will all

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:58.310
be affected by this
specific group.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:01.910
So why is this really
important for Shiny?

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:06.650
The thing is, every web page right
now in some way or another uses

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:08.950
css and Shiny
is no exception.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:12.020
So even though you're
programming an area

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.410
and you're building
this Shiny application,

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:17.330
behind the scenes,
what's actually happening

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.180
is that Shiny is
generating some Javascript,

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:22.100
some HTML, some css.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.100
And the key part
here is the css.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.710
So because some css
is being generated,

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:31.370
we can also add our
own on top of it.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.680
So how would we actually
go about doing this?

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.920
So with Shiny itself, there's
three very specific ways

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:41.870
that you can do it.

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:45.000
So you can add the styling
directly to your tags

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.830
when you're building
your UI. You can add it as,

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:51.290
let's say, a separate
tag in the header

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:54.260
of your page or
your application.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:57.290
But you can also store it in files
and link those files directly

00:03:57.290 --> 00:03:59.990
to the Shiny application.

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:03.150
So for the first one.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.150
Whatever you create
in your UI, as long

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.850
as it's an HTML
tag, you can always

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:14.070
pass a style attribute
which lets you basically pass

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:19.550
any kind of css statement that will
affect that specific element.

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:20.769
So here

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:24.090
there's no statements, there's
no classes, there's nothing.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:25.590
This is style that
you want to apply

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:30.340
to that specific to
that specific element.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:36.460
And this is fine, this
is OK, but to be honest,

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:40.000
this is probably, this should
be your last resort when

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.580
it comes to adding css to Shiny.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:46.370
The truth is that
by adding, it's fine

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.760
if you have one or two elements,
but when you get to 10,

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:52.750
20, 50 elements, then you
start having very complex UIs.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:56.740
You lose track of where you
styled the specific thing.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:59.360
You cannot reuse any rules.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.650
So if you have three titles, you
need to style the three titles.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:04.510
It's very hard to
keep consistency

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:07.130
between all the elements.

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:10.900
This is something that if you
use bslib, for example,

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:14.140
it's hard for you because
there's general rules that

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:15.400
affect every element.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.470
But if you're doing
these individual changes,

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:20.320
you won't be able to
actually keep track of them

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:23.440
as your project grows.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:26.320
The second way would
be to add css to your headers.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:28.480
So this is slightly better.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:34.370
This is you can add a style
tag with some text inside,

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:37.000
and that text can be css text.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.820
This is slightly better.

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:42.350
This does mean that you
can use those selectors.

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:45.730
You can apply the
same styling to a lot

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:47.350
of elements at the same time.

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:51.040
But at the same time,
you won't really

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:54.040
be able to leverage
the browser here

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:57.550
because you're generating
the css whenever

00:05:57.550 --> 00:05:58.730
the page is created.

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:02.080
This actually means
that your browser

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:04.000
won't be able to cache these.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:07.570
So by caching, I mean
saving them for future usage

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:10.600
when you reopen the
application later.

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:14.770
So you end up having a
slightly better approach,

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:18.550
but at the same time, it's
still not the ideal approach.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:21.830
So we can always do
the third option.

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:25.060
So this would be to
create a file with all

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:28.840
of our css inside,
link this file

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:31.360
to our Shiny
application, and then

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.580
the browser actually
has something that it

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:35.410
can save for the future.

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:37.990
And at the same time,
we have a specific place

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:41.770
where we have all our
styling neatly saved away

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:44.560
from our code, from our logic.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:46.150
This is much better.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:49.820
This is probably the
best, the best approach

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:50.390
you can take.

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:53.260
if you're using
just regular css.

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:54.910
You can reuse the
code, you can use

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:59.050
the rules, you can cache
these files in your browser,

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:01.270
can leverage the
cache to actually save

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:02.980
these files for later.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:06.670
It also allows you to
separate your styling

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:09.730
into multiple files and

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:14.290
atart adding a bit of
structure to how you're saving,

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:17.710
styling for different elements,
for different modules.

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:20.200
However, css
is still something

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:21.970
that can get very complex.

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:25.720
And it's kind of this thing
that grows out of control very

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:27.640
fast as your project grows.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:30.260
So there's still a bit of
room for improvement here.

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:36.870
So one way that you can improve
this workflow even better

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:37.900
is to use SASS.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:45.090
So sass is, as Tom mentioned
before, this is a language

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:46.560
built on top of css.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:50.820
And the idea here is that
it's preprocessor to css.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:54.240
So any sass that
you create always

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.430
ends up being css.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:58.950
But there's this layer
of abstraction where

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:01.380
you have a few different tools.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:03.000
Then you don't
really need to worry

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.420
about creating very complex or
managing very complex css

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:09.240
because sass just gives
you a couple of ways

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:10.890
of managing it a bit better.

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:14.100
So a couple of differences
here between sass and css

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:16.740
are sources are object oriented.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:19.720
So this means that
you can have nothing.

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:21.600
It's more about
the concept of what

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.360
is a specific element that
you're trying to target,

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:27.090
not the specific element
that you're targeting.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:28.560
It also allows variables.

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.610
I know that css community is
actually pushing forward

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:34.250
with variables in css.

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:37.929
This is, I think,
a very nice bonus.

00:08:37.929 --> 00:08:41.280
But it's one of the
things that sass,

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:44.039
at least in my
understanding, still does much

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:45.990
better than just the css.

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:48.990
And also, you can always
use sass to actually generate

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:50.930
variables, two or three
years in the future.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:56.568
So a couple of really

00:08:56.568 --> 00:08:58.360
interesting things that
you can do with sass,

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:01.040
so we talked about nesting.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:02.860
So what nesting
means is that you

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:06.940
can simplify a lot of the rules
that you do by nesting them.

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:10.810
So as you can see css
on the right side,

00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:12.210
doesn't really allow for this.

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:15.190
So if you're targeting two
different elements in this

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:18.610
case, for example, a UL and
an LI elements inside

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:20.830
of a nav bar, you're going
to need to create the full

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:21.520
selecter

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:26.350
every time you create a rule.
Sass kind of solves this

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:27.460
by allowing nesting.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:33.490
So if you have two elements
inside the same element,

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:36.460
you can kind of nest them
together and simplify

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:40.300
your code even a tiny bit more.

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:44.930
We also talked about variables.
So css does allow variables,

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:48.760
but sass does it in a way
that you can kind of

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:51.750
declare them, even in separate
files, completely different,

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:54.700
there is no scope required,
which is something

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:57.010
that css variables need.

00:09:57.010 --> 00:10:01.990
And you can simplify them by
assigning a name to a property

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:04.250
that starts with a dollar sign.

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:06.580
And then just this
becomes a variable

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:10.000
that you can use
throughout your code.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:14.530
It also has two very
important mechanisms

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:16.610
which are extends and mixins.

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:20.200
And so extends, this
kind of, and this

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:23.380
is something that is very well
explained with an example.

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:26.860
So it basically
allows you to share

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:29.440
styles between
selectors and the way

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:31.780
that you would do this
normally in css

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:34.870
if you would have two elements
that you would like to style,

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:36.310
even though they
are very similar,

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:39.730
is that you would simply do
the styling for both of them.

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:40.960
By using extend

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:44.560
you can actually create
a partial part of code

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:50.440
by using the percentage
as a marker for the name

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:52.060
of that block of code.

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:53.830
And you can then

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:57.160
extend for each
element, you can simply

00:10:57.160 --> 00:11:02.800
extend the name, the extend
rule that you created,

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:06.190
and the code will actually
be copied into that selector.

00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:10.390
So this means that you
can reuse parts of styling

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:14.310
that you use very
often by simply

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:20.070
extracting them into a separate
name that you can then reuse.

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:21.390
the second one is mixins.

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:24.030
So mixins are
slightly similar,

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:27.630
but these days work
more like functions.

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:31.470
So the idea here is that
instead of simply taking out

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:35.100
a piece of a block of code
and saving it with the name,

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:37.210
with a mixin and you're
actually having a function.

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:39.990
So this means that you can
pass specific arguments

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:43.430
to those functions
that you're creating.

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:45.990
So in this case,
for example, we've

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:48.840
identified the only difference
between these two components

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:50.670
is actually the smaller size.

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:55.380
So we can create a mixin and that
has an argument of border size

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:59.040
and simply call those functions
in each of our declarations

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:02.550
for each specific element.

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:06.090
So small introduction to sass,
of a very big

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:08.640
a crash-course
kind of thing,

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:11.760
but hopefully it shows
you some of the advantages

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:13.020
that you actually have.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:16.920
So how do you actually
use this with R?

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:19.710
So the best way, of
course, to use the sass library

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:22.470
and RStudio was kind
enough to actually create

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:25.230
a really nice package for us.

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:31.500
So you can use R/sass
to not have to worry

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:33.280
about all the preprocessing.

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:34.590
You just need to write code.

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:36.450
This is all you want to do.

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:39.300
And in general, the
package gives you

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:41.040
one big function called sass,

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:45.570
and this function can take
either a string or a full file

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:47.470
and just return
the css to you.

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:51.840
So all of these techniques
that we've been talking about,

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:55.470
you know, have access to them
inside of R directly

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:59.060
without having to,

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:05.690
without actually having to
work outside of R files.

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:08.090
The second way is
actually you can actually

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:10.170
pass its reference to a file.

00:13:10.170 --> 00:13:12.200
So here, for
example, we're simply

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:15.470
pointing the cells function
to our style's main file.

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:19.310
And this file has a lot
of internal stuff for sass.

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:22.670
And this way, you can kind of file,

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:27.170
you can kind
of split all of your styling

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:30.950
into something that behaves
a bit more like code managing

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:35.000
and actually lets you
reuse a lot of the styles

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:38.060
and kind of keep a
grasp of styles when

00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:40.190
it comes to a very big project.

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:41.957
A second argument
that's very important.

00:13:41.957 --> 00:13:43.290
And this function is the output.

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:46.820
So the output will generate
that css to a specific file.

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:50.090
And then this is something that
you can simply add to your UI.

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:56.120
And now you have all of your
styles saved in a project,

00:13:56.120 --> 00:14:00.020
in a project folder
structure that

00:14:00.020 --> 00:14:05.300
has everything well sorted, then
you can kind of manage them.

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:07.910
If you would like to
see an example of sass

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:11.130
being used very extensively,

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:16.140
I will point you to my Shiny
contest entry from last year.

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:18.060
I used sass quite a lot,

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:20.830
and hopefully it will
guide you in the right way

00:14:20.830 --> 00:14:24.410
to get started.

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:26.730
And I think my 15
minutes are up,

00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:30.330
and I'd just like to
thank you for this.

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:33.690
And I also I know that the
slides will be available later.

00:14:33.690 --> 00:14:36.860
So I've also added a
couple of resources

00:14:36.860 --> 00:14:39.560
here to get you started with sass.

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:44.450
And in case, you really
want to get into css,

00:14:44.450 --> 00:14:46.280
there's also a couple
of bonus slides

00:14:46.280 --> 00:14:50.390
with some of the
selectors that I

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:53.930
think are at least the
basic ones that you need to

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:56.180
for css.

00:14:56.180 --> 00:14:59.070
So hopefully this
will get you started.

00:14:59.070 --> 00:15:02.300
And in one year,
you'll be thinking, how

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:06.460
have I been using css without sass?

00:15:06.460 --> 00:15:09.550
So let's talk about speeding up Shiny apps.

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:15.880
So small disclaimer, this is
going to be just some of the,

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:18.730
I think, very important
details that you always

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:20.170
need to take care of.

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:23.500
It doesn't mean that you'll
be able to speed up everything

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:27.250
using just the steps,
but hopefully it

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:32.890
will get you started when it
comes to at least identifyng

00:15:32.890 --> 00:15:36.220
some of the issues that
your Shiny app might have.

00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:39.130
So I'm going to talk about three
very important details when

00:15:39.130 --> 00:15:41.380
it comes to speeding up
Shiny applications.

00:15:41.380 --> 00:15:43.770
The first one is
update functions.

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:48.100
I'll also talk a bit about proxy
objects and proxy functions.

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:52.300
And then I will finish up by
letting you know a bit about custom

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:54.350
messages and offloading.

00:15:54.350 --> 00:15:56.240
I won't get into
too much detail.

00:15:56.240 --> 00:15:58.270
And of course, every
package is different

00:15:58.270 --> 00:16:01.870
and needs different
types of optimizations.

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:05.310
But hopefully this will
help you get started.

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:09.540
So update functions,
what are these functions?

00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:12.970
So update functions are

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:15.850
very simple
functions that

00:16:15.850 --> 00:16:19.780
let you update
existing Shiny widgets,

00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:24.760
so the important thing here
is that base Shiny has these,

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:28.690
but it's very possible that
you're not always using them.

00:16:28.690 --> 00:16:34.180
So as an example, we have here
a very simple Shiny application

00:16:34.180 --> 00:16:36.730
that has just a
checkbox and a dropdown.

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:40.810
And you can see that in
our UI, we define checkbox

00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:42.760
and we define the UI outputs.

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:46.420
And then in our server
we simply observe

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:51.070
that a checkbox, and we
change our select input label

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:57.160
and values depending on
the state of the checkbox.

00:16:57.160 --> 00:16:58.530
This is very standard.

00:16:58.530 --> 00:16:59.730
This is simple.

00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:02.410
This is something
that you probably did

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:04.569
quite a lot of times already.

00:17:04.569 --> 00:17:07.359
However, you can see
that we're not really

00:17:07.359 --> 00:17:09.160
using any update functions.

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:11.589
So what do I mean by that?

00:17:11.589 --> 00:17:14.859
What's happening here
is that every time we

00:17:14.859 --> 00:17:17.200
trigger a change
with the checkbox,

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:19.510
we're actually re
rendering the dropdown.

00:17:19.510 --> 00:17:24.890
So you can see that because
we're using a UI output

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:29.450
and then filling in that UI output
with our dropdown, what's

00:17:29.450 --> 00:17:31.040
actually happening
behind the scenes

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:35.660
is that whenever this
output changes, Shiny is

00:17:35.660 --> 00:17:38.960
removing all the
bindings that it creates

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:40.070
is in the background.

00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:46.270
It creates a new set of bindings
for a new dropdown that just,

00:17:46.270 --> 00:17:49.460
it does have the same idea, but
it's a completely new dropdown

00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:54.570
and has to rebuild the
whole structure, so this,

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:56.160
we can probably do better here.

00:17:56.160 --> 00:17:58.610
And this is where update
functions come in.

00:17:58.610 --> 00:18:02.420
So if we simply

00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:05.810
change our codes to
instead of using a UI

00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:09.770
outputs to simply
render select inputs,

00:18:09.770 --> 00:18:13.820
and then instead of
filling in the UI output,

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:18.140
we simply use the update
select inputs function.

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:20.120
This is completely different.

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:21.740
So in this case,
what's happening

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:24.890
is that there's a call
from Shiny being sent

00:18:24.890 --> 00:18:30.590
to the browser that says, hey,
update this specific input

00:18:30.590 --> 00:18:34.880
that already exist in the
browser with a new label

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:36.680
and some new values.

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:40.280
So you can see here that
these are very small changes,

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:44.930
but it's already

00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:47.360
slightly different approach
to how we're actually

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:49.790
building the application.

00:18:49.790 --> 00:18:51.500
Does this really matter?

00:18:51.500 --> 00:18:57.740
And just to give you an example
of what kind of improvements

00:18:57.740 --> 00:19:00.740
we're talking about,
the difference even

00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:05.210
for this simple drop
down, is actually

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:08.330
that for the page
to be completely re

00:19:08.330 --> 00:19:10.970
rendered and ready
for the user to use,

00:19:10.970 --> 00:19:15.860
it goes down from
15 milliseconds to 10

00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:16.970
milliseconds.

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:18.570
So just the simple,

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:21.980
and we're not even talking
about the very complex thing

00:19:21.980 --> 00:19:23.180
that we're doing here.

00:19:23.180 --> 00:19:25.310
It's just a simple drop down.

00:19:25.310 --> 00:19:26.910
It's already a
massive improvement.

00:19:26.910 --> 00:19:31.050
So you can imagine that
if we're talking about 50

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:34.670
drop downs, 50 elements that
will be re-rendering these really

00:19:34.670 --> 00:19:38.960
add up, add up very quickly.

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:41.600
So why is this actually better?

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:44.180
Like I said, the update
function is actually

00:19:44.180 --> 00:19:46.760
just sending a
message to the browser

00:19:46.760 --> 00:19:49.650
and then updating
that specific element.

00:19:49.650 --> 00:19:51.420
So there's no need
to rebuild anything.

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:54.590
It just uses the bindings
that Shiny already created

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:55.870
for this specific element.

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:01.340
But when it comes
to update functions,

00:20:01.340 --> 00:20:05.863
these are present in most
Shiny widgets out of the box.

00:20:05.863 --> 00:20:07.280
So if you're using
even just base

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:10.970
Shiny, any kind of
input that you create,

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:14.750
it probably has a
corresponding update function.

00:20:14.750 --> 00:20:18.960
So action buttons
always have an update

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:21.020
action button,
select inputs always

00:20:21.020 --> 00:20:22.860
have an update select input.

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:24.620
And you can see
here that there's

00:20:24.620 --> 00:20:29.990
a bit of a standard where
the update function is always

00:20:29.990 --> 00:20:35.150
the name of the UI definition
of the widget with a prefix

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:37.380
update.

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:40.940
Of course, always make sure
to check the documentation.

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:46.020
Usually these are linked
the bottom after the function

00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:48.000
definition, you have
some information

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:50.460
about what kind of
updates actually exist

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:52.590
and what kind of
arguments can actually

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:56.130
be updated with that function.

00:20:56.130 --> 00:21:00.250
So these update
functions are nice,

00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:04.190
but what happens when we have
slightly more complex widgets?

00:21:04.190 --> 00:21:05.860
And this is the case
of when you have,

00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:12.220
for example, the DT table or an E-Charts chart,
or a Leaflet map, these all

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:13.960
work slightly
different because we're

00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:19.300
talking about very complex,
very complex widgets,

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:22.420
that a simple update
function is probably not

00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:24.490
enough for everything.

00:21:24.490 --> 00:21:26.180
So this is where
proxys come in.

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:31.030
So proxys are basically a
way to reference an existing

00:21:31.030 --> 00:21:33.370
widget, an existing
complex widget,

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:36.580
and then usually they
have their own set

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:42.770
of functions that behave
similar to update functions

00:21:42.770 --> 00:21:46.080
but are applied to that
specific instance,

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:49.670
in the proxy, the instance
of the widget itself.

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:55.680
So a lot of what usually happens
is that, an example is DT.

00:21:55.680 --> 00:22:00.890
So you render, you create
your data table outputs,

00:22:00.890 --> 00:22:04.310
you fill it in with
render data table.

00:22:04.310 --> 00:22:08.090
It creates a table
by using the way

00:22:08.090 --> 00:22:10.280
to access the proxy
to that element

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:13.320
that we just created is actually
to use data tables proxy.

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:16.310
So this is a function
that also exists and lets

00:22:16.310 --> 00:22:20.150
you save a reference
to a specific table

00:22:20.150 --> 00:22:23.390
with a specific id that
was defined somewhere else.

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:29.640
In this case, it uses the same
id as the data table output.

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:33.510
And you can see that
this is basically

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:35.460
an example of how
you could use proxy,

00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:39.720
so we define our data table
output, we fill it

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:45.120
in with our render table
and some, in this case,

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:46.620
the iris data set.

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:50.820
And then if we
save the reference

00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:53.550
to the proxy of that element
using data table proxy,

00:22:53.550 --> 00:22:54.850
we can just manipulate it.

00:22:54.850 --> 00:22:58.440
So in this case, we have a
small checkbox and every time

00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:02.490
the checkbox changes, we
call a specific proxy update

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:05.010
functions, which in this
case, is select rows

00:23:05.010 --> 00:23:08.070
and we just select or
don't select the first five

00:23:08.070 --> 00:23:08.940
rows of the table.

00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:15.990
So one important
thing to note here

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:20.960
is that different packages
also have different update functions.

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:25.740
So it's important to know to
figure out these functions

00:23:25.740 --> 00:23:27.210
whenever you need to use them.

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:30.090
Again, documentation
is always different.

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:35.540
So if you check for this data
table proxy function,

00:23:35.540 --> 00:23:38.520
you'll have a lot of
information about all the update

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:41.850
functions that come
with the proxy object.

00:23:41.850 --> 00:23:44.820
And internally
proxy actually works

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:48.150
very similar to the
update functions

00:23:48.150 --> 00:23:49.770
that I mentioned before.

00:23:49.770 --> 00:23:51.240
The difference here
is that instead

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:53.520
of having one
update function,

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:56.910
we actually have a family
of update functions

00:23:56.910 --> 00:23:58.380
and an object to apply to.

00:24:02.530 --> 00:24:06.040
Again it's important to
check the documentation,

00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:10.850
you won't always be able to
update every single thing,

00:24:10.850 --> 00:24:14.350
so it's very
important to know

00:24:14.350 --> 00:24:19.390
the family of proxy update
functions that exist.

00:24:19.390 --> 00:24:22.960
Hopefully there will be one
that does what you need it to do.

00:24:22.960 --> 00:24:28.300
If not, we'll probably
we'll actually talk about it

00:24:28.300 --> 00:24:30.820
now, which is custom messages.

00:24:30.820 --> 00:24:36.050
So if something doesn't really
exist, you can always create it.

00:24:36.050 --> 00:24:40.480
And this is part of the
beauty of Shiny generating

00:24:40.480 --> 00:24:44.410
HTML, css,  and JavaScript that you
were talking about before,

00:24:44.410 --> 00:24:48.110
because the fact that if

00:24:48.110 --> 00:24:50.020
you're still working
with the web pages, means

00:24:50.020 --> 00:24:56.410
that you can use JavaScript
by itself to actually augment

00:24:56.410 --> 00:25:00.020
Shiny, even more so.

00:25:00.020 --> 00:25:02.220
What are custom messages?

00:25:02.220 --> 00:25:06.930
So when I mentioned the update
functions, these functions

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:11.570
are basically a one way call
to the browser where internally

00:25:11.570 --> 00:25:16.280
there's some code being run,
something that you don't really

00:25:16.280 --> 00:25:18.920
know, but you know that it's
going to trigger something

00:25:18.920 --> 00:25:20.860
in the browser and update.

00:25:20.860 --> 00:25:22.430
What's actually
happening down

00:25:22.430 --> 00:25:25.520
there is similar to how
custom messages work.

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:30.050
So you can trigger JavaScript
functions from R directly

00:25:30.050 --> 00:25:34.340
or you can
trigger updates in Shiny

00:25:34.340 --> 00:25:36.250
from the browser.

00:25:36.250 --> 00:25:40.820
There's three
functions that kind of

00:25:40.820 --> 00:25:44.320
give you the whole
behavior of the messages.

00:25:44.320 --> 00:25:47.150
So in part, this is the
send custom message function.

00:25:47.150 --> 00:25:48.070
In JavaScript

00:25:48.070 --> 00:25:53.900
this is the add custom message
handler and on input change

00:25:53.900 --> 00:25:55.260
functions.

00:25:55.260 --> 00:25:58.590
So I will give you
another example

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:05.670
of shiny decisions, my Shiny
contest entry from last year.

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:07.740
So one of the things
that I had to do

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:11.700
was I had the
Leaflet map that was

00:26:11.700 --> 00:26:16.260
so I had the leaflet myself
that was displaying some markers

00:26:16.260 --> 00:26:17.490
is being updated.

00:26:17.490 --> 00:26:18.260
That's fine.

00:26:18.260 --> 00:26:20.610
There's always there's
an update function

00:26:20.610 --> 00:26:23.760
for a leaflet that lets me
change the data that way.

00:26:23.760 --> 00:26:28.440
But I wanted to have this extra
behavior of actually changing

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:31.500
the saturation of
the map depending

00:26:31.500 --> 00:26:34.050
on how bad you're
actually you're

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:36.540
actually doing in the game.

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:41.650
So I. Isn't something that
is standard to leaflet.

00:26:41.650 --> 00:26:43.690
This had to be custom
built, and this

00:26:43.690 --> 00:26:47.740
is where custom messages
kind of came in. So

00:26:47.740 --> 00:26:49.500
from the JavaScript side,

00:26:49.500 --> 00:26:53.530
remember, there's the
add custom messenger

00:26:53.530 --> 00:26:55.960
and custom message
handler function.

00:26:55.960 --> 00:26:58.030
And basically what
this does is it

00:26:58.030 --> 00:27:05.920
lets you run a function whenever
a specific id is

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:07.000
triggered in Shiny.

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:11.770
So what I mean by that is
that it kind of registers

00:27:11.770 --> 00:27:16.060
the handler so that it can
be triggered from Shiny.

00:27:16.060 --> 00:27:20.230
So in this case, I had this
update map style handler that

00:27:20.230 --> 00:27:24.070
will call this update map style
function and the update map

00:27:24.070 --> 00:27:27.140
function, it will
create some css

00:27:27.140 --> 00:27:33.370
that kind of gives you
that your sepia filter that

00:27:33.370 --> 00:27:37.780
gets a bit more evident
whenever your scores are

00:27:37.780 --> 00:27:40.820
very low in the game or
it goes up, whatever.

00:27:40.820 --> 00:27:42.370
And goes back to
the normal colors

00:27:42.370 --> 00:27:43.930
whenever you're doing better.

00:27:43.930 --> 00:27:46.850
So now we have some
JavaScript behavior.

00:27:46.850 --> 00:27:49.870
And we have a Shiny handler
to trigger that behavior.

00:27:49.870 --> 00:27:51.730
So on the R
side, we just need

00:27:51.730 --> 00:27:54.640
to send the custom
message for the same id

00:27:54.640 --> 00:28:00.280
with whatever values we want
to pass to the JavaScript

00:28:00.280 --> 00:28:01.480
function.

00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:04.300
So two simple functions,
and we already

00:28:04.300 --> 00:28:07.670
have a leaflet behavior that
doesn't really exist before.

00:28:11.500 --> 00:28:15.400
As far as a very small
proof of concept,

00:28:15.400 --> 00:28:20.420
the way, this is actually
working is that from the way,

00:28:20.420 --> 00:28:22.810
the whole thing works,
is that from R you're

00:28:22.810 --> 00:28:26.770
sending the custom message
that the message is looking

00:28:26.770 --> 00:28:29.740
for a specific handler,
that handler is triggering

00:28:29.740 --> 00:28:31.240
a specific JavaScript function.

00:28:31.240 --> 00:28:32.050
That's it.

00:28:32.050 --> 00:28:35.930
There's no magic here.

00:28:35.930 --> 00:28:41.430
So now we can from Shiny,
trigger things in JavaScript.

00:28:41.430 --> 00:28:47.520
But what happens if we want
to do the opposite thing?

00:28:47.520 --> 00:28:50.320
So we actually want to.

00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:53.670
And I just realized these
are actually created.

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:56.560
So the first one is
actually JavaScript.

00:28:56.560 --> 00:28:58.720
The second one is
actually Shiny.

00:28:58.720 --> 00:29:01.200
So if we have a
JavaScript function

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:04.520
that's doing some
work in the browser.

00:29:04.520 --> 00:29:08.420
How do we actually tell Shiny
that something is happening,

00:29:08.420 --> 00:29:10.080
that something happened?

00:29:10.080 --> 00:29:11.860
So we can use this input value.

00:29:11.860 --> 00:29:14.120
So this input value
works very similar.

00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:17.450
It has an ID and a
message and what it does

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:20.270
is that it sends a
message to Shiny

00:29:20.270 --> 00:29:24.920
that can be observable in the
list of inputs in the Shiny

00:29:24.920 --> 00:29:25.610
session.

00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:30.080
So this means that our
input value will actually

00:29:30.080 --> 00:29:34.550
be changed into the message
that we pass from JavaScript.

00:29:34.550 --> 00:29:37.940
So we can just observe
it, do whatever we need,

00:29:37.940 --> 00:29:43.840
so we can pass messages
from the browser back to Shiny.

00:29:43.840 --> 00:29:47.920
And another example
of something that

00:29:47.920 --> 00:29:50.290
uses this kind of behavior
in the same application,

00:29:50.290 --> 00:29:55.040
so here I was observing whenever
the user made a choice.

00:29:55.040 --> 00:29:59.177
And then I was just sending
using this input values

00:29:59.177 --> 00:30:00.760
so that I could
observe it in Shiny

00:30:00.760 --> 00:30:04.430
and actually know that
something changed.

00:30:04.430 --> 00:30:06.230
So why are these
important when it

00:30:06.230 --> 00:30:11.150
comes to speeding up Shiny? so because
these allow you to actually

00:30:11.150 --> 00:30:14.600
pass a lot of the time to run
whatever you want directly

00:30:14.600 --> 00:30:15.240
in the browser.

00:30:15.240 --> 00:30:18.470
This means that you can free
your shiny process to actually

00:30:18.470 --> 00:30:20.820
do only the important things.

00:30:20.820 --> 00:30:25.340
So if you have very small, very
small changes that are just

00:30:25.340 --> 00:30:28.220
UI changes and don't really
need to go to the server.

00:30:28.220 --> 00:30:30.590
This is one way that
you can think of them.

00:30:30.590 --> 00:30:34.400
It also means that if you have
some kind of code that already

00:30:34.400 --> 00:30:36.290
exists in Javascript,
you can actually

00:30:36.290 --> 00:30:38.120
just trigger that
code instead of having

00:30:38.120 --> 00:30:39.420
to rebuild it in Shiny.

00:30:39.420 --> 00:30:44.090
So this speeds up not only Shiny
itself, but actually the Shiny

00:30:44.090 --> 00:30:45.830
development, because
you won't need

00:30:45.830 --> 00:30:51.580
to just redo the same function
again in a different language.

00:30:51.580 --> 00:30:54.142
It also allows you to
extend these functions

00:30:54.142 --> 00:30:55.600
that we talked
about before, simply

00:30:55.600 --> 00:31:03.070
because there's no need to
write the corresponding Shiny

00:31:03.070 --> 00:31:04.630
function, the
corresponding update

00:31:04.630 --> 00:31:06.220
function, if this
is something very

00:31:06.220 --> 00:31:08.100
specific to your application.

00:31:08.100 --> 00:31:13.150
So it's very easy to get
started and very powerful.

00:31:13.150 --> 00:31:15.370
Of course, keep in
mind that you will

00:31:15.370 --> 00:31:18.790
need to know a bit of a Javascript
at least to get started.

00:31:18.790 --> 00:31:21.130
But if you don't
know it by now, I'd

00:31:21.130 --> 00:31:23.860
definitely recommend you to at
least know some of the basics,

00:31:23.860 --> 00:31:28.960
because it's very, very
powerful when it comes to Shiny.

00:31:28.960 --> 00:31:31.540
And that's it from my part.

00:31:31.540 --> 00:31:32.180
Thank you.

00:31:32.180 --> 00:31:36.150
If you have any
questions, just reach out.

